194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418387 1069 "q-182642" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418387 1071 "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all {{field-9}}." 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418387 1072 #uuid "07e17262-4a40-5650-84e4-b70a5816bde7" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418387 1074 "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every natural number $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every natural number $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)=2(3)+3=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+6)(3-2)=9(1)=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=3,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)=(k+6)(k-2).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=(k+7)(k-1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & =(k+6)(k-2)+(2k+5) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+4k-12+2k+5 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+6k-7 \\\\  & =(k+7)(k-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all $n\\ge 3.$" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418374 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418375 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418380 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418381 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1069 "q-182681" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1072 #uuid "fb1ce6e5-5388-5457-9a90-11e65945cce7" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418388 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)=1(5-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)=k(5k-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3) & =(k+1)(5(k+1)-1) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5k+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418383 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418389 1069 "q-182715" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418389 1071 "If $S(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(2-i),$ then $S(k+1)=$" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418389 1072 #uuid "fdbaf03b-caeb-5592-aca9-e0d9ca517487" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418389 1074 "We need to express the summation $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$\n\nLet ${a}_{i}$ for $i\\ge 0$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k+1$ terms.\n\n$$\nS(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i}\n$$\n\nWe can always express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k)$ by writing the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${a}_{i}=2-i.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1}=2-(k+1)=1-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}(2-i)=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(2-i))+(1-k),\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as \n\n$$\nS(k+1)=S(k)+1-k.\n$$" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418390 1058 #edb/ref 17592186418392 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418390 1062 #uuid "459c5a5c-82ad-5383-9f14-b32d642843f8" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418390 1063 #edb/ref 17592186418387 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418391 1062 #uuid "ebf63460-3ab8-5dad-9770-7340329a4251" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418391 1063 #edb/ref 17592186418389 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418392 1058 #edb/ref 17592186418391 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418392 1062 #uuid "f2f4d29e-fc61-5451-bca5-1fd86de9ebbb" 13194139534309 17592186045417 true] [17592186418392 1063 #edb/ref 17592186418388 13194139534309 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-e0ad2bc8-002f-5f86-8ec2-7e277d1905c2" 17592186418319 "answer-02a6e75d-f947-51ea-82be-89ed67ffed13" 17592186418320 "answer-170b5188-e4be-5c5a-bed2-81603245e803" 17592186418321 "answer-18860e2f-7cd5-5b29-8d68-36637e4ab199" 17592186418322 "answer-195ef169-fd55-5c12-96db-aacd2f818443" 17592186418323 "answer-1def3558-6632-56fc-8010-d375b6430e0e" 17592186418324 "answer-20878a90-2c36-577a-bc96-7f696c09ee15" 17592186418325 "answer-2205f4da-a260-5351-a36a-5b08e10ef0bf" 17592186418326 "answer-29d77c33-04b2-5acc-89d3-67d91419edd8" 17592186418327 "answer-43e4a339-e05e-5b3b-99f5-a4b53b17019f" 17592186418328 "answer-5179fa85-5800-5d8a-825e-19f954d6e613" 17592186418329 "answer-53222015-2d0c-56a2-aefb-c91299c9f701" 17592186418330 "answer-5a169e12-7e4c-5e25-8147